{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And the one did not come near the other.</b> Rashi explained: one camp to the other camp. And by way of allusion one may interpret it concerning Israel, for our Sages of blessed memory said (Yerushalmi Ta'anit 2:5) that four factions arose among our fathers at the sea, and they were not of one accord — this one says thus and that one says thus; therefore 'the one did not come near the other all the night' in their minds. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 23:7) that the angels did not say song all the night, as it is said of them (Isaiah 6:3) 'and one called to the other,' and in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer: do not read 'the one did not come near (לֹא קָרַב) the other' but rather 'did not call out (לֹא קָרָא).' And there is no need to emend the text, for the angels do not draw near to one another for any matter in the world except at the time they say songs and praises before Him, blessed be He — one calls and the other answers opposite him. And when it says 'the one did not come near the other,' surely they did not say song, for the way of song is that one calls and the other answers." "And one may say further: 'and the one did not come near' — that is, the angel mentioned above in the verse 'and the angel of Elohim journeyed' (Exodus 14:19), to 'this' — that is, to the song alluded to in the word 'this' as it says 'זֶה אֵלִי וְאַנְוֵהוּ' (Exodus 15:2), for that is the essence of the song, that they merited to see the face of the Shekhinah until they were pointing at Him with a finger and said 'this is my El.' For a maidservant at the sea saw what Ezekiel did not see. And this is an allusion to the angels as well, for among them the nearness of apprehension is more prevalent than among those below."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:11.044973524Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}